    "content": "The 2026 \"Dirty Dozen,\" released by the Environmental Working Group (EWG), highlights produce with the highest pesticide residues, topped by spinach, kale/collard/mustard greens, strawberries, grapes, and nectarines. Other high-residue items include peaches, cherries, apples, blackberries, pears, potatoes, and blueberries. Over 60% of these samples contained PFAS \"forever chemicals\". EWG Dirty Dozen 2026 List According to the 2026 report, these items contain the most pesticide residues: Spinach (highest residues by weight) Kale, Collard & Mustard Greens Strawberries Grapes Nectarines Peaches Cherries Apples Blackberries Pears Potatoes BlueberriesAdditional high-residue items: Bell and hot peppers, green beans. Key Findings & Advice Contamination Rates: 96% of Dirty Dozen samples contained pesticides, with many containing four or more different types. PFAS Detected: For the first time, the 2026 report found that over 60% of samples contained PFAS \"forever chemicals,\" which take decades to break down. Actionable Advice: The EWG recommends buying organic versions of these items when possible. How to Clean: Thoroughly wash all produce. A 30-second soak in a solution of 2 teaspoons baking soda and 1 quart of water is more effective at removing pesticide residue than water alone. The \"Clean Fifteen\" (Lowest Pesticide Residue) For items with the least pesticide residue, you may not need to buy organic. The top \"Clean Fifteen\" items for 2026 include: Avocados Sweet corn Pineapple Onions Papaya Frozen peas Asparagus Honeydew Kiwi Cabbage Mushrooms Mangoes Sweet potatoes Watermelon Carrots Note: Some experts, like those at The Garden Professors, argue that the EWG list overstates risks by ignoring the low levels of actual exposure. The Garden Professors",
